2. Key housing characteristics
2.1. More than 22,500 housing units were recorded in Monaco in 2025
Beyond counting residents, the census also aims to count the housing units used for residential purposes
in a territory. The register -based approach makes it possible to obtain this information and covers those
built by 31 December 2025, thus excluding bui ldings still under construction or those that had been
demolished by that date.
Table 10. Number of housing units by district in 2024 and 2025
Source: Monaco Statistics - Population censuses
In 2025, the Principality counts 22,577 housing units, up 1.8% year on year.
Figure 17. Distribution of housing units by district in 2025
Note: The districts are those defined by Sovereign Ordinance No. 4.481 of 13 September 2013. “Ravin Sainte-Dévote” has been
included in the “Les Moneghetti” district.
Source: Monaco Statistics – 2025 Population census
Monte-Carlo and La Rousse together account for more than 10,200 housing units, or 45.4%. Larvotto and
Monaco-Ville are the districts with the fewest dwellings, with 1,391 and 615 respectively.
The other four districts: “La Condamine, Jardin Exotique, Les Moneghetti, and Fontvieille” each have
between 2,200 and 2,900 housing units.
